{
 "experiment": "localization_targeted_attacks",
 "date": "2026-08-31",
 "framing": "E3 split the loss into disjoint buckets at the estimated pose: 22.5% where the truth is not among the candidates at all (P1), and 45.0% where it is a candidate but is not picked (P2). These are attacks on P1 and P2 specifically, not further ceiling measurements.",
 "P2_ranking_rules_on_identical_shortlists": {
  "oracle_truth_in_top25": 0.5833333333333334,
  "rules": {
   "peak": {
    "correct": 21,
    "rate": 0.35
   },
   "sum": {
    "correct": 4,
    "rate": 0.06666666666666667
   },
   "peaky": {
    "correct": 15,
    "rate": 0.25
   },
   "votes": {
    "correct": 17,
    "rate": 0.2833333333333333
   },
   "pk_over_std": {
    "correct": 0,
    "rate": 0.0
   }
  },
  "finding": "Every alternative rule is worse than ranking by peak: votes 28.3%, peakiness 25.0%, sum 6.7%, peak/std 0.0%, against 35.0% shipped. Cross-pose evidence fails as a shortlist tie-break exactly as it failed as a global argmax."
 },
 "P1_why_the_truth_is_absent": {
  "n_miss": 25,
  "local_max_r1": 10,
  "median_percentile": 80.1739,
  "finding": "10 of 25 misses are suppression artifacts -- the truth is still a local maximum at r=1px but is absorbed by a stronger neighbour inside the NMS window. This is a dead end for scoring because the GLOBAL ARGMAX IS NMS-INDEPENDENT: widening the shortlist cannot change rank-1, and re-ranking the wider list does not work. The substantive finding is that the truth's median percentile on the surface is 80.2% among failures -- a fifth of the entire surface outscores it, so the defect is the true site's SCORE, not its rank."
 },
 "score_attack_robust_similarity": {
  "hypothesis": "Occlusion and local damage dilute the true match: plain ZNCC averages every pixel, so damaged pixels penalise the true site while a lattice alias, never expected to match there, pays nothing. A trimmed similarity should rescue it.",
  "rules": {
   "peak (shipped)": {
    "correct": 21,
    "rate": 0.35
   },
   "trimmed ZNCC, drop 10%": {
    "correct": 20,
    "rate": 0.3333333333333333,
    "mcnemar_p": 1.0
   },
   "trimmed ZNCC, drop 25%": {
    "correct": 19,
    "rate": 0.31666666666666665,
    "mcnemar_p": 0.75390625
   },
   "trimmed ZNCC, drop 40%": {
    "correct": 18,
    "rate": 0.3,
    "mcnemar_p": 0.548828125
   },
   "inlier fraction": {
    "correct": 18,
    "rate": 0.3,
    "mcnemar_p": 0.5078125
   }
  },
  "finding": "REFUTED, and informatively so. Trimming hurts monotonically (35.0 -> 33.3 -> 31.7 -> 30.0% as the dropped fraction goes 0 -> 10 -> 25 -> 40%), and no arm approaches significance. If damage were localised, discarding the worst-agreeing pixels would help. That it never does means the disagreement is spread uniformly across the template, which is the signature of independent acquisition noise rather than occlusion. This is positive corroboration of the mechanism identified in samecanvas_bound.json."
 },
 "conclusion": "Seven targeted attacks, each aimed at a specific measured defect, all negative: oracle pose (+6 pp), cross-pose accumulation in three forms (far worse), dense periodic residual (p=1.00), five shortlist ranking rules (all worse), NMS widening (structurally cannot help), and four robust/trimmed similarities (monotonically worse). Combined with the ten earlier families this is seventeen independent negative results converging on one mechanism. The shipped rule -- global argmax of band-passed ZNCC over the 45-pose sweep -- is at a local optimum, and the binding constraint is that the reference and search are independent acquisitions.",
